% ============================================================================
% electoral-college-count — the numbers USA.gov states about the U.S.
% Electoral College (adj-facts-stdlib, CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% "How many electoral votes do you need to win?" is a question with an
% exact, checkable answer, and exactly the kind an engine should return
% from a citation rather than from a model's recollection of a number.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? electoral_college_count(electors_needed_to_win, $N)
%       % 270
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `electoral_college_count(quantity, count)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the number WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any quantity not one of these four. The query
% also runs BACKWARD, binding the quantity from the number:
%
%     ? electoral_college_count($Q, 538)
%       % total_electors
%
% NINTH library in `civics/`, and the first about elections themselves
% rather than the branches of government or the path of a bill. A
% full-tree grep for `elector`/`electoral`/`electoral_college` across every
% shipped `.adj` returned nothing beforehand. (The numerals 538 and 270 do
% appear elsewhere in this stdlib — as a count of infant bones and inside
% NCBI Bookshelf identifiers — which is coincidence, not coverage; a future
% reader grepping for the bare numbers will hit those.)
%
% THE FIRST COLUMN NAMES WHAT IS COUNTED, because the units differ. Three
% rows count ELECTORS and one counts STATES, so a bare `count` column would
% be ambiguous on its own; the quantity atom carries the unit
% (`total_electors`, `winner_take_all_states`) rather than leaving a reader
% to infer it from context.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USA.gov's "Electoral College" page:
%
%     quantity (this table's atom)     count   quote (verbatim)
%     -------------------------------  -----   -------------------------------------------------------
%     total_electors                     538   "Including Washington, D.C.'s three electors, there are
%     district_of_columbia_electors        3      currently 538 electors in all." (one sentence states
%                                                 both numbers)
%     electors_needed_to_win             270   "A candidate needs the vote of at least 270 electors—
%                                                 more than half of all electors—to win the
%                                                 presidential election."
%     winner_take_all_states              48   "In 48 states and Washington, D.C., the winner gets all
%                                                 the electoral votes for that state."
%
% curl-fetched the raw page and read it byte-for-byte before writing this
% file (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch); the URL was reachability-
% checked from this machine before any scoping work, and the em dashes in
% the 270 sentence were confirmed to be U+2014 rather than hyphens so the
% quote is byte-faithful. `trust authoritative` — USA.gov is the U.S.
% General Services Administration's official guide to government
% information, a first-party .gov publisher of exactly the fact being
% recalled, the same tier the eight sibling civics libraries cite.
%
% A NOTE ON "CURRENTLY". The source says "there are CURRENTLY 538 electors
% in all", and that hedge is real: the total tracks congressional
% apportionment and D.C.'s three electors, so it is stable but not
% constitutionally fixed. The row records what the source states, and the
% verbatim sentence carrying the word "currently" travels with every
% answer, so a reader sees the qualifier rather than inheriting a bare
% number presented as timeless. (Compare `veto-override.adj`, where the
% source's "in most cases" is kept inside the recalled atom itself; here
% the hedge attaches to the whole count rather than to a distinguishable
% value, so carrying it in the citation is the faithful placement.)
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - Maine and Nebraska "assign their electors using a proportional
%     system" — a METHOD, not a count, and the reason those two states are
%     excluded from the 48. Different axis, its own future table.
%   - The three components the page lists for the process (selection of
%     electors, meeting of electors, counting of the votes by Congress) are
%     a list of what the process INCLUDES, not counts, and the page does
%     not number them; tabling them as an ordered sequence would assign
%     positions the source never states (see `bill-stage-successor.adj`'s
%     header for the case that established this rule).
%   - "This has happened twice" (no candidate winning a majority, sending
%     the vote to the House) and the 2016/2000 popular-vote outcomes are
%     counts of HISTORICAL OCCURRENCES, a different subject from the size
%     of the College. Not rows here.
%   - The penalties a "rare elector who votes for someone else" may face —
%     fined, disqualified, replaced, prosecuted — are consequences, not
%     counts. Their own future table.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table electoral_college_count {
    columns quantity, count

    row (total_electors, 538)
    row (district_of_columbia_electors, 3)
    row (electors_needed_to_win, 270)
    row (winner_take_all_states, 48)

    source "Including Washington, D.C.'s three electors, there are currently 538 electors in all."
    locator "https://www.usa.gov/electoral-college"
    trust authoritative

    cites "A candidate needs the vote of at least 270 electors—more than half of all electors—to win the presidential election." locator "https://www.usa.gov/electoral-college"
    cites "In 48 states and Washington, D.C., the winner gets all the electoral votes for that state." locator "https://www.usa.gov/electoral-college"
}
